What is derived
---------------

Kumo Vue is an independent implementation for a different framework. No
Kumo source file is copied verbatim: Kumo is React and Tailwind, this is
Vue and plain CSS, so every file here was written against Kumo's design
rather than transcribed from it. What is derived is the design itself.

In packages/tokens - the vocabulary of the token layer:

  * The names of the semantic colour tokens and the roles they denote -
    surface, text, status, border and interaction roles such as `canvas`,
    `elevated`, `recessed`, `subtle`, `hairline` and `fill-hover`.

  * The light and dark assignments for most of those roles, expressed
    against this project's own primitive scales.

  * The type size scale (12 / 13 / 14 / 16px), and the heading steps
    above it (20 / 24 / 30px).

In packages/ui - component design, behaviour and appearance:

  * Autocomplete. Its size scale, the free-form value contract that
    distinguishes it from a combobox, the popup and item dimensions, and
    the label / description / error field arrangement.

  * Badge. Its variant vocabulary (semantic `primary` / `secondary` /
    `error` / `warning` / `success` / `info` / `outline` / `beta`, plus
    the colour palette and the `dot` appearance), its dimensions, and
    the rule that only four variants carry a status dot.

  * Banner. Its variant and size vocabulary, its dimensions, and the
    structured title / description / action layout.

  * Breadcrumbs. Its size vocabulary (`sm` / `base`) and row heights,
    the chevron separator, the rule that ancestors do not shrink while
    the current crumb truncates, the collapse to the last two crumbs and
    an ellipsis on narrow viewports, the loading skeleton on the current
    crumb, and the hover-revealed deeplink copy button.

  * Checkbox. Its dimensions and radius, the contrast fill and inverse
    tick of the checked and indeterminate states, the extended pointer
    target around the box, the control-first / label-first layouts, the
    error ring, and the group's legend / description / error
    arrangement.

  * Tabs. Its two variants and their dimensions, the sliding indicator,
    the size scale, the horizontal overflow behaviour - drag to scroll,
    the fading scroll controls at a clipped edge, and moving by whole
    tabs - and the choice to render a tab bar with no panels.

  * Toast. Its variants and their icons, tints and accent rings, the
    dimensions and corner of the viewport, the collapsed stack that
    expands on hover, swipe-to-dismiss, the actions row and the
    corner close control, and the manager's `add` / `update` /
    `promise` shape including the bump-on-duplicate rule.

  * Text. Its variant vocabulary (`heading`, `body`, `secondary`,
    `success`, `error`, `mono`, `mono-secondary`, and the deprecated
    `heading1` / `heading2` / `heading3`), the size each variant renders
    at, the rule that monospace sits one step below the copy beside it,
    the separation of visual variant from rendered element, and the
    truncation behaviour.

  * Select. Its size scale, trigger and popup dimensions, the option
    and group-label styling, and the required / optional label
    convention.

  * Button. Its variant, size and shape vocabulary (`primary`,
    `secondary`, `ghost`, `destructive`, `secondary-destructive`,
    `outline`; `xs` / `sm` / `base` / `lg`; `base` / `square` / `circle`),
    the dimensions of each size, the emphasis gradient treatment, and
    behaviours such as swapping a disabled link for a real button and
    requiring an accessible name on icon-only buttons. Kumo's Tailwind
    utility classes were translated into CSS custom properties; the React
    implementation was not translated line by line.

Files carrying derived content have a one-line header pointing here.

The intent is that a developer familiar with Kumo finds the same words
and the same behaviour. Where this project diverges - the line-height
scale, the brand colour, the emphasis gradient range, and several values
adjusted to meet WCAG AA - those departures are documented in
packages/tokens/README.md and packages/ui/README.md.

Cloudflare's brand colours and trade marks are not used in this project
and are not covered by the MIT grant above. The brand token ships as a
neutral placeholder for consumers to override.

